# Writing Rules

## The core principles

> "Lots of people raise formality, and thus indirectness, when they should use plainer language. Fewer and common words make for the most direct writing."

Formal contexts call for clarity. Plain words, short sentences, no padding. A proposal addressed to an executive should be *more* direct than a Slack message, not less.

> There's nothing I hate more than pretty words that don't actually say anything, or glance off of the substantive core.

Be specific and clear, even if it takes more words to get there. Jargon, cliche, and businessspeak reflect half-formed thoughts; avoid these in favor of naming the thing in plan language.

Every sentence must earn its place with something specific. If a sentence could appear in any document, about any topic, cut it or fix it. Vagueness is deadly.

## Voice

- Start in the middle of things. No throat-clearing, no "In this document, I will..."
- Specific over vague. "Eleven years at Belkin" not "many years of experience." "410 lbs" not "a heavy lift."
- Active voice as the default. Passive when the actor genuinely doesn't matter.
- Short declarative sentences. Trust the full stop.
- Anecdote → insight is a reliable structure for explanatory writing.
- First person is fine. Use the active voice. "I think" is honest. "It is believed that" is cowardly.
- Exclamation points are allowed in casual/personal writing. Use them when something actually warrants them.
- Parentheticals work for asides, qualifications, or a wry note, but they accumulate. One per paragraph is probably the ceiling. If it could be its own sentence, make it one.
- Fragments work for emphasis. Use them. The problem is the rhetorical triplet — three consecutive fragments as a device ("Fast. Focused. Intentional."). Use the right number of things.
- Contractions are natural. Use them.
- "We" language is inclusive. Prefer it over "you must" in instructional writing.
- When describing a quality or intention, name the mechanism, not the adjective. "Customer-responsive, quick and thoughtful" hands readers nothing to hold. What does it mean in practice? That's the sentence.
- Prefer the conversational word over the clinical or precise one when meaning is the same. "Tweak" beats "fix." "Believe" beats "trust" (when used abstractly). "Just as a person does" beats an impersonal construction. Writing in a personal, direct register means making these micro-choices consistently.
- In opinion pieces, give the argument you care most about the most room. If a paragraph represents your core point — not a cite, not a setup, your actual view — expand it. Don't compress what matters. Readers read what you wrote; they can't see what you cut.

## Tone for internal comms

- Blend company context with personal framing when cascading leadership messages. Leads with the "why" before the "what."
- Creates psychological safety without being soft on expectations. Starts conversations rather than closing them.
- When your team is part of a cross-functional problem, name the shared failure honestly, then assign your team's specific piece of it. Don't make them spectators critiquing a peer team.
- Closing lines should tell the reader what kind of room to expect, not what disposition to bring. "Open mind" is a disposition; "come with something prepared" is a behavioral signal.
- Pre-reads should be scannable. Target 400–700 words.


## Formatting

Context-dependent — prose first, structure only when it genuinely helps.

- Short messages and casual writing demand prose, no headers, no bullets unless listing is the actual point.
- Long documents and guides need headers and bullets to guide the reader, especially when the content is genuinely list-like or the reader needs to scan.
- Use bold sparingly and for genuinely critical information, not for decoration.
- Use space en dash space – like this – not crunched, and not em dashes. Before reaching for one, consider breaking the sentence, using a colon, or using a semicolon.
- Don't use inline-header vertical lists (the pattern where every item is **Bold label:** followed by text). More broadly: bullets without surrounding prose lose your voice. If you can't write one sentence before and one after a list, the list probably isn't ready.
- After a list, say what the items add up to. Don't let structure replace the argument.
- The right number of things is the right number — not the rhetorically tidy number.

## Punctuation

- Oxford comma: always. ("red, white, and blue")
- Numbers: spell out one through nine; numerals from 10 up.
- En dash with spaces on both sides – like this – not crunched, not em dashes.
- Standard double quotation marks for quotes; single for quotes within quotes.
- Semicolons for joining independent clauses. En dashes for sharp interruptions or true asides — and sparingly.
- Parallelism in series: if you use "how" before the first item, use it before all items, or restructure.

## Phrases and patterns to avoid

### Design-world clichés

- **"surface" as a verb** — "This surfaces the question," "design both surfaces." It's everywhere in design discourse, which is exactly why it reads as lazy. Use "reveals," "exposes," "makes visible," or restructure.

### Empty assertion

- **"X is real"** — "The external context is real." Asserts a condition without naming what makes it true. Just say the thing.

### False binaries

- **"It's Y, not X"** and **"Not X, but Y"** — Usually there's a more precise way to say it. Just say it, don't make a weak contrast meterly for effect.

### Preambles and throat-clearing

"I've been thinking about…", "I wanted to…", "I've been meaning to…" Cut them. Start with the substance.

### Corporate / meeting culture

- leverage *(as a verb — "we can leverage this")*
- circle back
- reach out *(just "contact" or "ask")*
- moving forward / going forward
- stakeholders *(usually means "people")*
- ensure *(usually means "make sure" or "check")*
- utilize *(means "use")*
- commence *(means "start")*

### Filler and hedging

- very, really, quite, literally *(empty intensifiers)*
- currently *(usually redundant — if it's true now, just say so)*
- genuinely, honestly, straightforward *(used as emphasis words, not substantive)*
- as a matter of fact
- in fact
- that said
- it is noted that / it is evident that / it is well known that / it is a fact that / it has been found that / it has long been known that / it may be said that / it is possible that
- in my opinion *(just make the claim)*
- allegedly *(unless you mean it in the legal sense)*
- type of *(usually vague filler)*

### Throat-clearing phrases

- It is important to note that... *(just say the thing)*
- It is interesting to note that... *(ditto)*
- It is our opinion that... *(just say "we think")*

